----- stdout -----
Record a new double-entry transaction.

Flags before the first --posting describe the transaction; flags after a --posting describe that posting, until the next one.

Usage: borrow-checker transaction add [OPTIONS] --date <DATE> --description <TEXT>

Options:
      --json
          Emit machine-readable JSON instead of human-readable output

          [env: BC_JSON=]

      --db-path <DB_PATH>
          Path to the SQLite database file.

          Overrides the `db_path` config file setting and the platform default. The `BC_DB_PATH` environment variable is also honoured via the config layer rather than directly by this flag.

  -v, --verbose...
          Increase log verbosity.

          Pass once for info (`-v`), twice for debug (`-vv`), three times for trace (`-vvv`). Use `RUST_LOG` for fine-grained per-crate control.

  -q, --quiet...
          Decrease log verbosity.

          Pass once for error-only output (`-q`). Use `RUST_LOG` for fine-grained per-crate control.

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Transaction and posting flags:
      --date <DATE>
          The transaction's date (YYYY-MM-DD). Goes before the first posting.

      --description <TEXT>
          The transaction's description. Goes before the first posting.

      --posting <ACCOUNT> [AMOUNT] [COMMODITY]
          Open a new posting on ACCOUNT (path or ID). Without AMOUNT and COMMODITY its amount is elided. Posting flags after it apply to it.

      --meta <KEY=VALUE>
          Metadata entry for the transaction, or for the posting it follows. Repeat for several; a key may repeat.

      --tag <TAG>
          Tag the transaction, or the posting it follows. TAG is an ID or a path; a missing path is created.

      --cost <AMOUNT> <COMMODITY>
          Per-unit cost basis of the posting it follows.

      --total-cost <AMOUNT> <COMMODITY>
          Total cost basis of the posting it follows.

      --lot-date <DATE>
          Lot date of the posting's cost.

      --lot-label <LABEL>
          Lot label of the posting's cost.

      --price <AMOUNT> <COMMODITY>
          Per-unit price of the posting it follows.

      --total-price <AMOUNT> <COMMODITY>
          Total price of the posting it follows.

      --spread <FROM> <UNTIL>
          Accrue the posting it follows over FROM..=UNTIL.
